{"step_index":360,"source":"MODEL","type":"PLANNER_RESPONSE","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-09-28T03:28:43Z","thinking":"Examining `split_fractal.lua`, the `main()` function's control flow depends on `CheckTimer()`. This function, located in `sf_logic.lua`, appears to manage a timer based on `os.time()` and an `end_time` variable. The current implementation truncates the last line of `CheckTimer()`. It looks like it should reset `end_time`, which is what I'll need to figure out.\n\n\nThe timer's reliance on `os.time()` creates a vulnerability to clock drift, potentially causing bar misalignment with the exchange grid. Using the real-time trade data's timestamp (`alltrade.datetime`) appears to provide a more reliable time source for the timer. Considering naming conventions, the new time variable representing the current tick's time should be named appropriately, considering the existing variables and relevant rules.\n\n\nConsidering that a new variable is needed, the relevant rule regarding variable declaration should be followed, which describes the required format for the declaration. Investigating the potential use of `alltrade.datetime`, it's critical to understand why similar code caused QUIK to crash previously. The issue appears to be related to the way this data structure is handled in the `message()` function; passing the `datetime` table directly, or accessing its members without a nil check, can crash the program.\n\n\nA robust method for extracting the timestamp from the trade data is needed, considering potential nil values. A double check on the type and presence of the `sec` field provides the safest extraction. Applying modulo arithmetic to the Unix timestamp and period time provides the start and end of the M5 bar grid, ensuring perfect alignment. User confirmation of an M5 period and request for time comparison is acknowledged.\n\n\n","tool_calls":[{"name":"run_command","args":{"CommandLine":"\"powershell -NoProfile -Command \\\"[Console]::OutputEncoding = [System.Text.Encoding]::UTF8; Select-String -Path 'Y:\\\\split_fractal\\\\*.lua' -Pattern 'tick_time'\\\"\"","Cwd":"\"Y:\\\\split_fractal\"","IsDaemon":"false","WaitMsBeforeAsync":"5000","toolAction":"\"Searching for tick_time\"","toolSummary":"\"Check if tick_time exists in split_fractal\""}}]}
